{"request": {"model": "gpt-6-astra", "effort": "low", "previous_response_id": null, "instructions": "You control a simulated Franka Panda robot arm on a table, by choosing end-effector waypoints.\nWorld frame, metres: the robot's base is at (-0.615, 0, 0) and faces +x; +y is the robot's left, +z is up.\n\nEach turn, answer with ONE JSON object:\n{\"description\": \"what happened in the latest frames and what you will do next\",\n \"waypoint\": [x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw],\n \"wait\": null,\n \"gripper\": -1, 0 or 1}\nThe robot moves its TCP (the point between the fingertips) to (x, y, z) with orientation (roll, pitch, yaw) in\nradians, using the same convention as the TCP pose reported to you, along a planned path; then it applies the\ngripper command: -1 = close, 1 = open, 0 = keep. Pointing straight down is roll = -3.1416, pitch = 0; yaw turns the\nhand about the vertical axis. If a pose cannot be reached the episode fails. A waypoint identical to the current\nTCP pose also fails: to only open or close the gripper, move the TCP by at least 2 mm (for example 5 mm up or down).\nTo wait instead of moving, answer \"waypoint\": null and \"wait\": the number of control steps to wait, a whole number\nfrom 1 to 300. Otherwise \"wait\" is null.\nIn this task the robot holds a stick instead of a gripper: the gripper command\nis ignored. Here the TCP is the lower free end of the stick, not a point between\nfingertips: a waypoint (x, y, z) puts the end of the stick there, and the\nreported TCP pose is where the end of the stick is.\nObservations: frames from the fixed front camera (256 x 256) in time order, one every 16 control steps\n(0.8 s) of the video so far and of each motion or wait, then the current front and wrist images; the TCP pose; the finger\npositions (0.04 m each = fully open, 0 = closed); the elapsed control steps; and the camera calibration: pinhole\nintrinsics K and world-to-camera extrinsics [R|t] (OpenCV convention: camera x right, y down, z forward;\npixel = K (R X_world + t), dividing by depth). You get no depth and no object positions. The task may require\nremembering what happened earlier in the video.\n\nNotes on the robot and this interface:\n- Time: the simulation runs at 20 control steps per second, and time passes only\n  while the robot executes a motion. A motion takes longer the farther it goes:\n  about 0.15-0.3 s for a few millimetres, 0.4-1.1 s for 1-5 cm and 1-2.5 s for\n  10-30 cm. Everything else in the scene keeps moving during a motion, so to act\n  at a particular moment, start the motion that much earlier. A small motion (a\n  few millimetres) is a short way to let a little time pass.\n- Gripper: it opens or closes only when you command it (-1 or 1); with 0 it\n  keeps its current state, also after touching, pushing or pressing something.\n- Waiting: an answer may also be a wait of k control steps. The robot then keeps\n  its pose and gripper while k steps pass, and the next message shows the frames\n  of the wait. Use a wait only when there is something to wait for.\n- Reaching: the TCP reaches the commanded position only approximately, typically\n  within about 2 mm after a short motion and within about 1 cm after a long one,\n  so after a motion the reported TCP pose is near the waypoint, not exactly at\n  it.\n- If an action executes no steps, the next message says so explicitly: then the\n  robot did not move, no time passed and the scene is unchanged.\n- Left and right: in task instructions, left and right are from the robot's point of view: the robot's left is +y and its right is -y. The front camera faces the robot (the robot is at the top of the front image), so the robot's right appears on the LEFT side of the front image.\nThe episode ends automatically when the environment judges success or failure, or after 1300 control steps.\nIf you believe the task is complete, answer with \"waypoint\": null and \"wait\": null.", "input": [{"type": "input_text", "text": "Task goal: watch the video carefully, then use the stick attached to the robot to navigate around the sticks on the table, following the same path"}, {"type": "input_text", "text": "Video so far: front-camera frames [0, 16, 32, 48, 64, 80, 96, 112, 128, 144, 160, 176, 192, 208, 224, 240, 256, 272, 288, 300] of 301 (last = current):"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "92826743d8c25c58e632c0688c0bfb816de48b9cf2408edaad256b43107b9e73"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "d654b373271a98f83624fdc52d131138930f653ab3764ce93f359bffab62a2d8"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "61104c4e596b9db9b2d92ac4d0e06331b47f8a2e062fd76bb6697b67eb468a87"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "ece77bc04255a38614da5447f5652f5c58bf3f7850130f9c075faf2b1adcd442"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "a205b23e389f10fbd3f9e6954aafed54e89bf7e8ccabf1f264769931d78aebb5"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "1876c2aa07255c9d0fa2ba998d4cc5737dd43bbd1e488ac6e9ab4ba93dbfe409"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "99489cb1af9799a5e665e11b7e8fe87eccd29b81c5d9bb31b45890613b77eec1"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "61ead954c67e2d20550c79d81849d267c854a77031de1e7efeb13da5c1a3f9b4"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "d3966f041076b32046d556bba6000fa5725073e8e1343bba1bd0a76023b3e00f"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "f229a8629f71bf5534590e3362ea30834e760ae3be0247e6a010b694e0375a86"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "20b2d7fbed9d4b5aa287300ec3e5b342b3c25ba056dded0ad56a2c04fc188af6"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "b1432a5ea7d6f828a323473c2e7a883ce210fd0c7855b7578ed87cf59713f1a9"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "1ba8978b7b55d1a8a10de77aa64bee560fd0fa125f84b77a83c6258fd88277a1"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "c4b85fd77b26a8764a9900f62f38acf3c4cbbd71345200e52f606b1f1086cef8"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "3ccab3890f0524cedc34cee6d7b54413e24f84a7420349c0b7abc6dbecc9c55d"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "49d702b86764e9dabeeba2cfca037f6e5792a928d8fb2983228e79714365993b"}, {"type": "input_image", "sha256": "b555